course: dictionary
lesson: 8412
title: Rung: a step and a verb
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A rung is one of the bars or steps across a ladder. You put your foot on each rung to climb up or down. «Hold the ladder and step on each rung carefully.» Every rung holds up the next, so a ladder only works if all of its rungs are there.
explain: Rung is also a form of the verb ring: ring, rang, rung. Use rung after has, have or had: «The bell has rung.» Other verbs work in the same way: sing, sang, sung; swim, swam, swum.
explain: People also use rung for a step in a job or plan. «The first rung on the ladder» means the first step towards something bigger. Ben's teacher used a «lie ladder» to show this: one lie on the bottom rung needs more lies above it to hold it up.
explain: Context tells you which meaning fits. If the sentence is about climbing or steps, rung is the bar of a ladder. If it has has, have or had before it, rung is a verb. Try swapping in your meaning to check it makes sense.
fact: A rung is one bar or step of a ladder.
fact: Rung is also the past participle of ring: ring, rang, rung.
fact: «Hold on with one hand per rung» is a safety rule for climbing frames.
fact: «The first rung on the ladder» means the first step in a job or plan.
fact: DNA is shaped like a twisted ladder, and the order of its rungs is a recipe for building and running a body.
exercise: «Step carefully on each rung of the ladder.» What is a rung? | A road up a hill | *One bar or step of a ladder | A kind of rope | A loud bell
exercise: «The bell has rung.» What kind of word is rung here? | A kind of cake | A step on a ladder | A word meaning very old | *A form of the verb ring
exercise: Complete the family of ring: ring, rang, ___ | *rung | rings | rang again | ringing
test: «Ben climbed to the top rung of the ladder.» What does rung mean here? | *One step of a ladder | A loud noise | A kind of rope | A ring for a bell
test: «Sam has rung the bell twice today.» Which word is a form of ring? | Sam | *rung | today | bell
exam: Mum says, «Each text is one rung on a ladder back up.» What does rung mean here? | A bar on a climbing frame to hold | A form of the verb ring | *A step towards building trust again | A loud bell
